["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Quail find you with their eyes first and their ears second. A covey has many eyes and one lookout. Beat those and you get close.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart of how much each sense matters to a quail. Eyes: strongest, wide field and four colour cones, 70 to 85 percent. Ears: strong, best range about 1 to 4 kilohertz, 75 percent. Nose: weak in fowl, 70 percent. The covey adds many eyes and a sentinel on a high perch, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Which sense does a quail trust?</text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Eyes</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"58\" width=\"270\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Ears</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"104\" width=\"220\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Nose</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"150\" width=\"50\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">weak (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"235\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">strongest (70 to 85%)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">strong (75%)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"196\" width=\"380\" height=\"94\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Plus the covey: many eyes at once,</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">and a sentinel on a high perch (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Beat the eyes first. Then the ears. (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Eyes: the strongest sense. Ears: strong. Nose: weak (70 to 85%).</li><li>A covey adds many eyes and a sentinel <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how sure is this chapter</summary><ul><li>Bird senses are well studied in general, less in quail. Where a number comes from the chicken or from birds in general, the screen says so (70 to 85%).</li><li>How to beat each sense is hunter craft, labelled Tip.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Top view of a bird head and its field of view. Each eye looks out to the side, so together they see almost all the way around. Only a narrow wedge in front, about 26 degrees in the chicken, is seen by both eyes, 70 percent. A small blind area sits behind the head. Birds have four colour cone types including ultraviolet, 85 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What a quail sees (top view)</text>\n<circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"110\" fill=\"#f3e3c3\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 165 L178 57 A110 110 0 0 1 222 57 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 165 L232 270 A110 110 0 0 1 168 270 Z\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"170\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"30\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"140\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/>\n<path d=\"M196 124 l4 -12 l4 12 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><circle cx=\"186\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"214\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Both eyes: narrow wedge (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Left eye</text>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Right eye</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Small blind spot behind</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"196\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4 colour cones</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"233\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">incl. UV (85%)</text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"196\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sees movement</text>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"233\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">all around (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Eyes on the sides of the head see almost all the way around <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Only a narrow wedge in front is seen by both eyes <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: vision field in full</summary><ul><li>In the chicken only about 26° straight ahead is seen by both eyes (binocular). The rest is seen by one eye at a time (70%, Poultry Hub).</li><li>In most birds the binocular field in front is narrow, about 20° to 30° at most (65%, Martin 2007 review, search preview).</li><li>A study of black grouse, a galliform cousin, found wide side fields and only a small blind area behind the head (65%, search preview).</li><li>So a quail feeding with its head down still watches most of the sky and the ground around it (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Hunter meaning: you cannot sneak up \"behind\" a quail the way you can behind a deer. Use cover and ground shape, not angles (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-quail-male-vernon.jpg\" alt=\"Bold face colours: quail read colour and pattern well.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bold face colours: quail read colour and pattern well. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan D. Wilson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_(Male).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Birds have four colour cone types, including ultraviolet (UV) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Movement gives you away more than colour (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: colour, ultraviolet and motion in full</summary><ul><li>Most birds carry four colour cone types: ultraviolet or violet, short, medium and long wave. Humans have three (85%, Stoddard et al. 2020, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences).</li><li>So a quail likely sees colours we cannot, including ultraviolet patterns <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Some hunters avoid clothes washed in brighteners that glow under ultraviolet light. No study on quail tests this: Tip only.</li><li>Blaze orange: birds see colour, but safety comes first. Wear it in brush (Tip, from the quick lesson).</li><li>Fowl are day active and need more light to see than some other birds (70%, Poultry Hub). Quail roost at dusk and leave the roost at first light <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Motion: a moving hunter on a skyline is the classic giveaway. Move when the sentinel is out of view, freeze when it looks (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Chart of bird hearing on a pitch scale from 0.1 to 20 kilohertz. Birds hear best from about 1 to 4 kilohertz, avian hearing reviews, 75 percent, and do not hear ultrasound. Human voices, boots on gravel and gun clicks fall in that range. Tip: whisper, use hand signals and close truck doors softly.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hearing: where birds hear best</text>\n<rect x=\"183.4140365931109\" y=\"70\" width=\"86.34385362755643\" height=\"90\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"226.5859634068891\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">best</text>\n<text x=\"226.5859634068891\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1 to 4 kHz</text>\n<line x1=\"40\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"40.0\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"40.0\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"40.0\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0.1</text>\n<line x1=\"140.24210977933268\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"140.24210977933268\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"140.24210977933268\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0.5</text>\n<line x1=\"183.4140365931109\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"183.4140365931109\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"183.4140365931109\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"269.7578902206673\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"269.7578902206673\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"269.7578902206673\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n<line x1=\"326.8280731862218\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"326.8280731862218\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"326.8280731862218\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10</text>\n<line x1=\"370.0\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"370.0\" y2=\"177\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"370.0\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">20</text>\n<text x=\"205\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">pitch, kilohertz (kHz)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"226\" width=\"380\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Voices, boots on gravel, gun clicks: all in range</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"274\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hush, hand signals, close doors softly (Tip)</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">low</text>\n<text x=\"370\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">high</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Birds hear best at about 1 to 4 kilohertz (kHz) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Voices, boots on gravel and gun clicks fall right in that band (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: hearing in full</summary><ul><li>Across many bird species, hearing is most sensitive from about 1 to 4 kHz (kilohertz), with a drop above that; no ultrasound (75%, avian hearing reviews including Dooling 1978).</li><li>Some songbirds are less sensitive than people in that band, so do not assume a quail hears better than you <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Quail live by sound: assembly calls, contact clucks and alarm calls keep the covey together (70%, Edgewood notes from Calkins et al.).</li><li>Cooper's hawks are known to hunt quail by their calls <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. Quail fall quiet when a hawk is about (Tip, 60%).</li><li>Hunter meaning: talk in whispers, use hand signals, close truck doors softly, and do not rattle shells in a pocket (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart of how much each sense matters to a quail. Eyes: strongest, wide field and four colour cones, 70 to 85 percent. Ears: strong, best range about 1 to 4 kilohertz, 75 percent. Nose: weak in fowl, 70 percent. The covey adds many eyes and a sentinel on a high perch, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Which sense does a quail trust?</text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Eyes</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"58\" width=\"270\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Ears</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"104\" width=\"220\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Nose</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"150\" width=\"50\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">weak (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"235\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">strongest (70 to 85%)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">strong (75%)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"196\" width=\"380\" height=\"94\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Plus the covey: many eyes at once,</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">and a sentinel on a high perch (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Beat the eyes first. Then the ears. (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Smell is not highly developed in fowl <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Wind still matters: for your sound and for a dog's nose (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: smell in full</summary><ul><li>In fowl the olfactory organ is not very highly developed, and smell seems secondary to other senses for finding food (70%, Poultry Hub).</li><li>No source says quail detect hunters by scent. Treat scent control as low value for quail (Tip).</li><li>Wind carries your sound. Walk into or across the wind so noise blows away from the cover ahead (Tip).</li><li>A dog finds quail by scent. Hunt so the wind blows from the cover to the dog (Tip).</li><li>Windy days: gray partridge shelter behind tree lines, fence rows, boulders and in lee draws (75%, Washington Fish and Wildlife).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation. A covey of quail feeds on open ground next to brush. A cock watches from the top of a fence post. You walk in from the left in the open. The sentinel sees you, gives the alarm, and the covey runs into the brush before you are close. Sentinel behaviour from bird guides, 70 percent. Lesson, a Tip: stay low, use cover and look for the bird on the post first.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The sentinel sees you first</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"220\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"200\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"40\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<rect x=\"196\" y=\"120\" width=\"8\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(200 110) scale(0.9 0.9)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -10 a7 7 0 0 0 9 6 L22 -2 a9 9 0 0 1 -9 -8 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M12 -9 q4 9 12 6\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-2 -10 8 -9 q-4 1 -5 9 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<g>\n<g transform=\"translate(150 200) scale(0.6 0.6)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-1 -6 5 -6 q-3 1 -3 6 Z\" fill=\"#4a4a4a\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(180 220) scale(0.6 0.6)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -10 a7 7 0 0 0 9 6 L22 -2 a9 9 0 0 1 -9 -8 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M12 -9 q4 9 12 6\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-2 -10 8 -9 q-4 1 -5 9 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(230 212) scale(0.6 0.6)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-1 -6 5 -6 q-3 1 -3 6 Z\" fill=\"#4a4a4a\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(260 196) scale(0.6 0.6)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -10 a7 7 0 0 0 9 6 L22 -2 a9 9 0 0 1 -9 -8 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M12 -9 q4 9 12 6\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-2 -10 8 -9 q-4 1 -5 9 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"4s\"/></g>\n<g><g transform=\"translate(0 0)\"><circle r=\"10\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"0\" y=\"-16\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You</text></g><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"20 230;100 220;100 220\" keyTimes=\"0;.35;1\" dur=\"9s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3.2s\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">!</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"4s\"/><circle r=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"><animate attributeName=\"cx\" values=\"150;330\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><animate attributeName=\"cy\" values=\"200;196\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></circle></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"4s\"/><circle r=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"><animate attributeName=\"cx\" values=\"180;344\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><animate attributeName=\"cy\" values=\"220;210\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></circle></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"4s\"/><circle r=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"><animate attributeName=\"cx\" values=\"230;316\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><animate attributeName=\"cy\" values=\"212;204\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></circle></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"4s\"/><circle r=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"><animate attributeName=\"cx\" values=\"260;352\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><animate attributeName=\"cy\" values=\"196;190\" begin=\"4s\" dur=\"2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></circle></g>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Covey feeds. A cock watches from a post.</text>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3.2s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">He calls the alarm. All run for the brush.</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"7s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lesson: stay low, use cover, look for the post</text></g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>The cock on the post sees you before the covey does. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>While the covey feeds, one bird, usually a male, watches from a high perch <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>He gives the alarm and the covey runs for cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the sentinel in full</summary><ul><li>\"During foraging one individual (usually male) often acts as sentinel, sitting on a high perch and giving Contact and Aerial Alarm Calls when it observes danger.\" (70%, Edgewood notes from Calkins et al. 1999)</li><li>The male acts as sentry throughout the year (70%, Birder's Handbook).</li><li>In the breeding season the male watches while the female sits on the nest <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Look for him first: a fence post, a stump, a rock or the top of a shrub (Tip). See <a href=\"#/s/habitat-upland\">the habitat tour</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-quail-vernon.jpg\" alt=\"A cock quail on a post: the covey's eyes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A cock quail on a post: the covey's eyes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan D. Wilson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_(Male).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Many eyes, one lookout, and calls to share the news <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Their answer to danger: run first, fly last <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: combining in full</summary><ul><li>A feeding covey watches in every direction at once. One alarm moves them all <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>They feed close to cover. California records say they seldom go more than 15 to 30 m (50 to 100 ft) from cover to feed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>With no hawks about they may feed 100 m from cover; under raptor pressure, about 15 m (70%, Edgewood notes).</li><li>First answer to danger: run into cover. They fly only as a last resort <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: they rarely let you see the whole covey in the open. Expect to hear them and to see a few birds on the edge (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Their edge</th><th>Your answer (Tip)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Eyes</td><td>Near 360° view, sentinel</td><td>Use cover and ground, freeze when watched</td></tr><tr><td>Ears</td><td>1 to 4 kHz <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td><td>Whisper, soft steps, hand signals</td></tr><tr><td>Nose</td><td>Weak <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>Wind for your sound and your dog</td></tr><tr><td>Covey</td><td>Many eyes, alarm call</td><td>Close fast at the end of cover</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Beat the eyes first, then the ears (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the plan in full</summary><ul><li>Approach with the sun behind you if you can: the bird looks into the light (Tip).</li><li>Stop often. Stopping makes nervous birds flush (75%, Washington Fish and Wildlife). See chapter 9.</li><li>The end of the cover is where they must fly. Be ready there (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Top view of a bird head and its field of view. Each eye looks out to the side, so together they see almost all the way around. Only a narrow wedge in front, about 26 degrees in the chicken, is seen by both eyes, 70 percent. A small blind area sits behind the head. Birds have four colour cone types including ultraviolet, 85 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What a quail sees (top view)</text>\n<circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"110\" fill=\"#f3e3c3\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 165 L178 57 A110 110 0 0 1 222 57 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 165 L232 270 A110 110 0 0 1 168 270 Z\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"170\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"30\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"140\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/>\n<path d=\"M196 124 l4 -12 l4 12 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><circle cx=\"186\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"214\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Both eyes: narrow wedge (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Left eye</text>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Right eye</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Small blind spot behind</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"196\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4 colour cones</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"233\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">incl. UV (85%)</text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"196\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sees movement</text>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"233\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">all around (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Eyes all round and ears to match; move slow and low to make the catch.\" (Tip)</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 4 cones, 1 to 4 kHz, 15 to 30 m from cover (80 to 85%).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li><strong>Post first:</strong> \"Find the post, find the ghost.\" Look for the sentinel before you look for the covey (Tip).</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule (opinion):</strong> \"Whisper in quail country.\" Hunting writers stress quiet: listen for the covey before it hears you (Tip; Missouri Conservationist 1996).</li></ul></details>","<ul><li>Poultry Hub Australia, Stoddard et al. 2020, avian hearing reviews, Martin 2007, Edgewood notes, California habitat records, Washington Fish and Wildlife. Checked 2026-10-11.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and date checked</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/nervous-systems-important-sensory-organs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Poultry Hub Australia: nervous system and sensory organs</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: 26° binocular field, light threshold, smell, ear <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7334476\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stoddard et al. 2020, Wild hummingbirds discriminate nonspectral colors, PNAS (PubMed Central)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: birds have four colour cone types <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Avian hearing: Dooling 1978 review in <a href=\"https://sora.unm.edu/sites/default/files/journals/auk/v095n03/p0563-p0568.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Auk 95: 563 to 568</a> and <a href=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/1kp2r437\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">What can birds hear? (eScholarship)</a>, search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://research.birmingham.ac.uk/en/publications/visual-fields-and-their-functions-in-birds\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Martin 2007, Visual fields and their functions in birds, Journal of Ornithology (University of Birmingham record)</a>, search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://friendsofedgewood.org/?p=5231\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Friends of Edgewood: California quail (Franks 2002, from Calkins et al. 1999)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: sentinel, calls, distance from cover, Cooper's hawks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://ncrcd.specialdistrict.org/files/b900df31f/California-Quail-info.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">California Wildlife Habitat Relationships: California quail</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/group/stanfordbirds/text/species/California_Quail.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford Birds (Birder's Handbook)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/node/68921\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Washington Fish and Wildlife: gray partridge hunting</a> and <a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/node/68963\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">quail hunting</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://mdc.mo.gov/node/245855\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Missouri Conservationist: Quail hunting without dogs (Smith 1996)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 (60%, bobwhite country).</li></ul></details>"]